\description{
Fits one generalized random forest for a binary exposure and returns, in
one object, the average treatment effect on an absolute or relative scale,
subgroup averages of the conditional average treatment effect (CATE), the
per-row CATE and doubly robust scores for drawing CATE curves, and the
fitted forest. Arguments follow \code{\link[RegR:get_eff]{RegR::get_eff()}} where the two overlap.
}
\section{Effect measures}{

Every measure compares the mean outcome of the two arms:\tabular{llll}{
   Outcome \tab \code{"diff"} \tab \code{"ratio"} \tab \code{"OR"} \cr
   continuous \tab mean difference \tab ratio of means (needs positive means) \tab -- \cr
   binary (0/1) \tab risk difference \tab risk ratio \tab odds ratio \cr
   survival, \code{target = "survival.probability"} \tab \eqn{S_1(t) - S_0(t)} \tab event-risk ratio \eqn{(1 - S_1(t)) / (1 - S_0(t))} \tab odds ratio of the event by \eqn{t} \cr
   survival, \code{target = "RMST"} \tab RMST difference \tab RMST ratio \tab -- \cr
}


\code{"diff"} is \code{\link[grf:average_treatment_effect]{grf::average_treatment_effect()}} itself. \code{"ratio"} and \code{"OR"}
come from arm-specific AIPW scores backed out of the forest, whose
difference reproduces \code{\link[grf:get_scores]{grf::get_scores()}} exactly. Their intervals and
p-values are Wald tests on the log scale with an influence-function
(delta-method) standard error, and \code{std.error} is reported on that log
scale. For a survival probability the relative measures compare the event
risk \eqn{1 - S(t)}, so a value below 1 favours treatment, as a hazard
ratio would, while \code{"diff"} stays on the survival scale of
\code{\link[RegR:get_eff]{RegR::get_eff()}}. A hazard ratio itself is not available: a causal forest
estimates contrasts of mean outcomes, which a hazard ratio is not.

Every measure needs overlap: if the propensity (\code{W.hat}, estimated or
supplied) is exactly 0 or 1 for any patient, the doubly robust scores are
undefined and the call stops. Propensities at or beyond 0.05 and 0.95 give
one warning with their range, which grf itself would repeat for every
estimand and subgroup.

Requested combinations that are not available -- \code{"OR"} for a continuous
outcome or RMST, a relative measure for anything but the ATE, a survival
estimand other than the ATE -- are skipped with a message; the call stops
only when none remains.
}

\section{Subgroups}{

\code{estimate} is the doubly robust effect within the subgroup:
\code{\link[grf:average_treatment_effect]{grf::average_treatment_effect()}} with \code{subset} for \code{"diff"}, and the arm
scores averaged over the subgroup for \code{"ratio"} and \code{"OR"}. \code{cate_mean} is
the estimand-weighted mean of the out-of-bag CATE in the subgroup, given
for \code{"diff"} rows only and only as a description: forest estimates are
shrunk towards the overall mean, so it carries no interval. \code{p_inter} tests
whether the subgroup estimates of one \code{sub_var} are equal (Wald
chi-square with K - 1 degrees of freedom, on the log scale for \code{"ratio"}
and \code{"OR"}). A subgroup in which an arm has no patient followed beyond
\code{time} gets \code{NA}, like one with fewer than two patients in an arm.
}

\section{CATE curves}{

\verb{$data} supports two univariate curves over a covariate \code{x}. Regressing
\code{.dr_score} on a smooth function of \code{x}, for example
\code{lm(.dr_score ~ splines::ns(x, 4), data = res$data)} with heteroskedasticity
robust standard errors, estimates \eqn{E[\tau(X) \mid x]} with valid
pointwise intervals. Plotting \code{.cate} against \code{x} shows the forest's own
out-of-bag estimates, whose smoother bands are not confidence intervals.
}

\section{Calibration}{

\verb{$calibration} is the calibration test of \code{\link[grf:test_calibration]{grf::test_calibration()}}: the
outcome residual \eqn{Y - m(X)} is regressed on \eqn{W - e(X)} times the
mean out-of-bag CATE and times each patient's deviation from that mean,
with grf's cluster-robust HC3 standard errors. A \code{mean.forest.prediction}
coefficient of 1 says the average CATE is right; a
\code{differential.forest.prediction} coefficient of 1 says its variation
around that average is well calibrated, and one significantly above 0 is
an omnibus test that the effect varies at all. \code{p.value} is one-sided
(coefficient > 0), as in grf. grf runs the test on a \code{causal_forest} only,
where \verb{$calibration} equals \code{grf::test_calibration(res$fit)}; for a
survival outcome the same regression runs on the censoring-adjusted
residual the \code{causal_survival_forest} keeps.
}

\examples{
\dontshow{if (requireNamespace("grf", quietly = TRUE)) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
\donttest{
set.seed(20260923)
n <- 800
d <- data.frame(age = rnorm(n, 60, 10), x2 = rnorm(n),
                sex = factor(sample(c("F", "M"), n, replace = TRUE)))
d$z <- rbinom(n, 1, plogis(0.3 * d$x2))
d$y <- rbinom(n, 1, plogis(-1 + 0.5 * d$x2 + d$z * (0.3 + 0.8 * (d$sex == "M"))))

# Binary outcome: risk difference, risk ratio and odds ratio, by sex
res <- get_hte(d, cat_var = "z", sub_var = "sex", adj_var = c("age", "x2"),
               surv = "y", measure = c("diff", "ratio", "OR"),
               grf_args = list(num.trees = 500, seed = 1))
res

# CATE curve over age: AIPW scores smoothed (valid intervals) and the
# out-of-bag CATE (descriptive)
curve_fit <- lm(.dr_score ~ poly(age, 3), data = res$data)
plot(res$data$age, res$data$.cate, xlab = "age", ylab = "CATE")

# Survival: S(t) difference and event-risk ratio at t = 60
ev   <- rexp(n, 0.02 * exp(0.3 * d$x2 - 0.5 * d$z))
cens <- pmin(rexp(n, 0.01), 120)
d$time <- pmin(ev, cens)
d$DSS  <- as.integer(ev <= cens)
get_hte(d, cat_var = "z", adj_var = c("age", "x2", "sex"), surv = TRUE,
        time = 60, measure = c("diff", "ratio"),
        grf_args = list(num.trees = 500, seed = 1))
}
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
}
